---
import ProductionComparison from "../components/methodology/ProductionComparison.astro";
import RelatedWorkTable from "../components/methodology/RelatedWorkTable.astro";
import SelectionFunnel from "../components/methodology/SelectionFunnel.astro";
import MethodOverview from "../components/methodology/MethodOverview.astro";
import { benchmark, bump, ecosystemLabel, highlighted, site } from "../data";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { formatDate, formatInteger } from "../lib/format";
import { authorLine } from "../lib/words";

const {
  release,
  configurations,
  metrics,
  model,
  budgets,
  pricing,
  production,
  selection,
  representativeness,
  paper,
} = benchmark;
const generatedAt = (iso: string) => `${iso.replace("T", " ").slice(0, 16)} UTC`;
const repoCounts = highlighted.ecosystems.map((e) => e.repos);
const { qualifying, composition } = selection;
const qualifyingSplit = Object.entries(qualifying.byEcosystem)
  .map(([id, n]) => `${n} ${ecosystemLabel(id)}`)
  .join(", ");
const languages = composition.byLanguage.map((l) => `${l.count} ${l.label}`).join(", ");
const millions = (n: number) => `${Math.round(n / 1e6)} million`;
const bumpRunDate = formatDate(bump.runDate);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description={`How ${release.name} chose its repositories, runs each upgrade, and verifies it through four gates.`}
>
  <MethodOverview />

  <section class="section method-detail" aria-labelledby="detail-title">
    <div class="container prose reading-width">
      <p class="eyebrow">The full method</p>
      <h2 id="detail-title" class="detail-title">Research question</h2>
      <p class="lede">{benchmark.researchQuestion}</p>
      <p>
        Software composition analysis tools report far more vulnerable
        dependencies than teams fix. In Snyk Open Source production data from
        {" "}{production.period}, {production.fixedVersionShare.toFixed(1)}% of
        findings have a known fixed version and {production.fixPathShare.toFixed(1)}%
        an upgrade path to it within the project, yet {production.openShare}% are
        still open, about {production.resolvedShare}% resolved and
        {" "}{production.ignoredShare}% ignored. The bottleneck is upgrading safely:
        an upgrade that removes the vulnerability but breaks the build or tests is
        not a fix.
      </p>
      <p>{benchmark.summary}</p>

      <h2>How VulnBump compares</h2>
      <p>
        A remediation benchmark needs real repositories with working builds and
        test suites, tasks driven by real vulnerability findings with known fix
        paths, and verification, independent of the agent, that the vulnerability
        is gone and the project still works. Existing benchmarks cover at most
        two: breaking-update benchmarks are not driven by vulnerabilities, so they
        cannot observe an agent that leaves the vulnerability in place, and CORAL
        scores against its own CVE-to-version mapping rather than a production
        scan.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="container wide">
      <RelatedWorkTable />
    </div>

    <div class="container prose reading-width">
      <h2>The dataset</h2>
      <h3>Grounded in production data</h3>
      <p>
        The target distributions come from Snyk Open Source findings in monitored
        projects from {production.period}: about
        {" "}{formatInteger(production.organisations)} organisations and
        {" "}{millions(production.issueProjectPairs)} unique issue–project pairs. A
        Snyk project is one monitored manifest, and a repository can contain
        several; because each project must be fixed separately, a vulnerability
        counts once for every project it affects. Java (62.7%), JavaScript (22.1%) and
        Python (9.9%) account for almost 95% of findings, and Maven, Gradle, npm,
        Yarn, pip and Poetry cover about 92% of all pairs, so VulnBump targets
        these six.
      </p>

      <h3>Candidates</h3>
      <p>
        Candidates come from {selection.sources.length} sources, which together
        cover all six package managers:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          selection.sources.map((s) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{s.label} ({formatInteger(s.count)}).</strong> {s.description}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each candidate is scanned with <code>snyk test --json</code>, recording
        severity, fixability, direct or transitive status and fix difficulty per
        unique issue–project pair. Some repositories were collected at several
        commits, giving {formatInteger(selection.collected)} candidates from
        {" "}{formatInteger(selection.collectedRepos)} repositories; keeping the most
        vulnerable commit of each leaves {formatInteger(selection.candidates)}: one
        per repository plus the 46 Gradle submodules.
      </p>

      <h3>Filtering for reproducible, well-tested projects</h3>
      <p>
        So that any later breakage can be attributed to the upgrade, the
        unmodified project must install, build and pass its tests from a clean
        checkout in its ecosystem's image (JDK 17, Node 20 or Python 3.11), with a
        30-minute limit per step. Each candidate had to pass five filters in
        order; the first requires every declared dependency to download and
        install with the project's own package manager, without private
        registries. The largest drop is at the third: many projects have no tests
        that run in a clean environment, and most pip projects need external
        services such as Postgres or Redis. Earlier failures were mostly
        ecosystem-specific: multi-module resolution, private registries and
        plugins (Maven, Gradle), peer-dependency conflicts (npm), and lockfile or
        Python-version mismatches (Poetry). This leaves {qualifying.candidates}
        {" "}qualifying candidates from {qualifying.repos} repositories
        ({qualifyingSplit}).
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="container wide">
      <SelectionFunnel />
    </div>

    <div class="container prose reading-width">
      <h3>Selection and final composition</h3>
      <p>
        {selection.selectionNote} The dataset comprises
        {" "}{highlighted.ecosystems.map((e) => `${e.repos} ${ecosystemLabel(e.id)}`).join(", ")}
        {" "}repositories ({languages}), with a median of {composition.medianVulns}
        {" "}unique vulnerabilities (range {composition.vulnRange[0]}–{composition.vulnRange[1]})
        and {composition.medianTests} passing tests; {composition.popular} have at least
        1,000 GitHub stars.
      </p>
      <p>{selection.excludedReason}</p>

      <h3>Against production</h3>
      <p>
        The dataset matches production within a few points on severity, fix
        difficulty, and package-manager share except for Gradle, and on direct
        share for Maven, npm, Yarn and Poetry. Vulnerabilities per project span a similar range for Java and
        JavaScript, while the Python repositories cluster around a higher median.
        There are two deviations:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {representativeness.deviations.map((d) => <li>{d}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="container wide">
      <ProductionComparison />
    </div>

    <div class="container prose reading-width">
      <h2>The task</h2>
      <p>
        Each task asks an agent to apply one package upgrade (or pin) recommended
        by a baseline Snyk scan (<code>snyk test --all-projects --ignore-policy</code>)
        of the installed, pinned repository, which must first install, scan,
        build and pass at least one test at evaluation time. Snyk groups the
        vulnerabilities it
        can fix into fix groups: one upgrade or pin in one manifest, with the
        vulnerabilities it fixes. Vulnerabilities without a fix path are never
        counted against an agent.
      </p>
      <dl class="definitions">
        <div>
          <dt>Upgrade</dt>
          <dd>
            A VulnBump task: one fix group of a pinned repository. A repository
            with 50 fixable vulnerabilities that require upgrading 20 packages
            yields 20 upgrades.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Fixable vulnerability instance</dt>
          <dd>
            A (manifest, vulnerability id) pair in the baseline scan for which the
            scan offers an upgrade or pin. An instance reachable through several
            upgrades counts as fixed only if every covering upgrade fixed its own
            path.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        An abridged baseline scan for one VulnBump repository (expressjs/generator)
        shows seven vulnerabilities across four upgrades. The minimist
        vulnerability is reachable through both the minimist and mkdirp upgrades,
        so it counts as fixed only if both fix their paths:
      </p>
      <pre class="listing"><code>Tested 9 dependencies for known issues, found 7 issues, 8 vulnerable paths.

Issues to fix by upgrading:

  Upgrade ejs@2.6.2 to ejs@3.1.10 to fix
  ✗ Improper Control of Dynamically-Managed Code Resources [Medium][SNYK-JS-EJS-6689533]
  ✗ Remote Code Execution (RCE) [High][SNYK-JS-EJS-2803307]

  Upgrade minimatch@3.0.4 to minimatch@3.1.3 to fix
  ✗ Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) [Medium][SNYK-JS-MINIMATCH-3050818]
  ✗ Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) [High][SNYK-JS-MINIMATCH-15309438]
  ✗ Inefficient Algorithmic Complexity [High][SNYK-JS-MINIMATCH-15353389]

  Upgrade minimist@1.2.5 to minimist@1.2.6 to fix
  ✗ Prototype Pollution [Low][SNYK-JS-MINIMIST-2429795]

  Upgrade mkdirp@0.5.1 to mkdirp@0.5.5 to fix
  ✗ Prototype Pollution [Low][SNYK-JS-MINIMIST-2429795]
  ✗ Prototype Pollution [Medium][SNYK-JS-MINIMIST-559764]
    introduced by mkdirp@0.5.1 &gt; minimist@0.0.8</code></pre>
      <p>
        Each upgrade is attempted from a fresh copy of the pinned repository and
        scored on its own (single-upgrade mode), so one upgrade's failure cannot
        affect another.
      </p>

      <h2>Harnesses</h2>
      <h3>The package manager bump</h3>
      <p>
        The non-agentic baseline applies Snyk's recommended upgrade and nothing
        else: it uses no model, edits no code or tests, and never tries another
        version. It sets the named package's version with the ecosystem's own
        mechanism (<code>npm install pkg@ver</code>, <code>yarn add</code>,
        {" "}<code>poetry add</code>, or the version in <code>requirements.txt</code>,
        {" "}<code>pom.xml</code> or the Gradle build), and pins an undeclared package
        the same way, for example with an explicit Maven <code>&lt;version&gt;</code>
        {" "}or a Gradle constraints entry. A pin is <em>managed</em> when it overrides
        a version set by a Maven parent or BOM or by Spring's
        dependency-management plugin. If the lockfile refresh fails, so does the
        dependency gate; the only fallback is retrying npm with
        {" "}<code>--legacy-peer-deps</code>. Python packages are pinned exactly
        (<code>==</code>); pinning with <code>&gt;=</code> changes the outcome of 2
        of the 60 Python upgrades and leaves the total unchanged. It ran on
        {" "}{bumpRunDate}, three days after the agents' scans, on the same pinned
        repositories.
      </p>

      <h3>The agents</h3>
      <p>
        All three agents use the same model ({model.id} on {model.provider}) and
        the same per-ecosystem container image. They differ in harness, prompt,
        tools and Snyk information, so VulnBump compares complete systems, as a
        user would get them, rather than ablating one component.
        {" "}{budgets.turns}
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Agent</th>
              <th scope="col">Harness revision</th>
              <th scope="col">Snyk CLI build</th>
              <th scope="col">Report generated</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Upgrades</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              configurations.map((c) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                    {c.label}
                    <small>{c.description}</small>
                  </th>
                  <td>{c.run.harnessRevision}</td>
                  <td>{c.run.cliBuild}</td>
                  <td>{generatedAt(c.run.generatedAt)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{formatInteger(c.attempts)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The Evo Remediation Agent is Snyk's agentic remediation engine, available
        through the Snyk CLI and a cloud service. It is invoked once per upgrade as
        {" "}<code>snyk fix --agentic --sca --auto-approve --prepare-env --issue-ids &lt;ids&gt;</code>,
        with the ids of the vulnerabilities that upgrade fixes. With
        {" "}<code>--auto-approve</code>, it applies every fix without prompting,
        including upgrades Snyk rates as likely to break the build, which a user
        could otherwise skip. It leverages Snyk intelligence, which evolved from
        Snyk's deterministic SCA fix engine for pull requests, and works from the
        same Snyk findings as Claude Code + Snyk scan. It runs its own scan, the
        build and tests, and repairs errors before finishing.
      </p>

      <h2>Sandbox and leakage controls</h2>
      <p>
        The agent runs in a restricted container modelled on the Evo Remediation
        Agent's production sandbox: a non-root user, a read-only filesystem except
        for the repository and a scratch directory, and 24 GB of memory. The
        ecosystem image (JDK 17 with Maven or Gradle, Node 20, or Python 3.11 with
        pip or Poetry) adds Node.js, a shell, <code>jq</code> and <code>yq</code>,
        and the project's dependencies are installed beforehand. Package
        registries are reachable (Maven Central through a read-through cache);
        Gradle wrapper distributions are pre-seeded because GitHub is blocked.
        Claude Code runs headless, without permission prompts, with its standard
        tools, including web fetch and web search; the Evo Remediation Agent has
        its own tools to read, edit and run commands, and no web tool. Three
        controls limit leakage of the eventual fix:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No history.</strong> Each repository is a depth-1 clone of the
          pinned commit, with no remote, refs or tags.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No GitHub.</strong> GitHub and mirrors that serve its repository
          files (jsDelivr, raw.githack, Sourcegraph) are blocked, and Claude Code
          has matching WebFetch deny rules and no <code>gh</code> CLI. Two
          repositories whose setup fetches from GitHub are exempt from the host
          block; only the WebFetch rules apply there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No Snyk for Claude Code unaided.</strong> It gets no Snyk CLI or
          token: snyk.io, security.snyk.io, api.snyk.io and app.snyk.io are
          blocked, and links to security.snyk.io are removed from its prompt.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The rest of the web, including changelogs and migration guides hosted
        outside GitHub, stays open; release notes hosted on GitHub are blocked
        with the rest of GitHub.
      </p>

      <h2>Verification</h2>
      <p>
        The verifier never trusts the agent's report. After the agent exits, it
        runs in a fresh container over the agent's working tree, as root with
        fresh caches and output directory, so nothing the agent left outside the
        repository (for example <code>PATH</code> shims or a local Maven
        repository) can affect the verdict. It runs all four gates in order, even
        when an earlier gate fails.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>{metrics.find((m) => m.key === "depsResolve")?.label}.</strong> The
          edited manifests and lockfiles install with the package manager's strict
          command, as CI would run it: <code>mvn dependency:resolve</code>,
          {" "}<code>npm ci</code>, <code>yarn install --frozen-lockfile</code>,
          {" "}<code>poetry install</code>, or <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>
          {" "}plus the project. Workarounds applied only during the agent's session do
          not count. Three npm repositories without a committed lockfile fall back
          to <code>npm install</code>. Gradle resolves dependencies during the
          build, so its resolution failures surface at the build gate; for Maven,
          which settles version conflicts itself, the gate is effectively a build
          precondition. If the
          strict install fails, npm and Yarn repositories fall back to a lenient
          install only so that the remaining gates can still run; the gate still
          fails.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rescan: targeted vulnerabilities fixed.</strong> In a post-fix
          {" "}<code>snyk test</code> of every target manifest, the targeted
          vulnerabilities count as fixed when they are no longer reachable through
          the upgraded package or through any newly introduced dependency path. A
          rescan that fails or omits a target manifest
          fails the gate. Both scans use <code>--ignore-policy</code>, so a
          {" "}<code>.snyk</code> file cannot hide a vulnerability.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Build.</strong> Maven and Gradle projects compile
          (<code>mvn compile</code>, <code>gradlew compileJava</code>); JavaScript
          projects run their TypeScript type-check (<code>tsc --noEmit</code>) or
          build script; Python projects import their main package, catching removed
          modules or symbols. Seven JavaScript repositories have neither a
          type-check nor a build script, so only their tests catch breakage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tests.</strong> Every test that passed in the baseline still
          passes; a skipped or missing test counts as a regression. Tests are
          matched by name from JUnit XML (Maven, Gradle, pytest) or Jest JSON
          reports. For the six Mocha-based npm repositories without JUnit output,
          the passing count in the log must not drop below the baseline's, and a
          log with no count fails.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        An attempt is a PASS only if all four gates pass. A gate that does not
        apply counts as passed. Agents may adapt tests to breaking changes
        (renames, API migrations, imports) but must not edit tests to make them
        pass.
      </p>

      <h2>Metrics</h2>
      <dl class="definitions">
        <div>
          <dt>Gate pass rates</dt>
          <dd>
            The share of the {formatInteger(highlighted.attempts)} upgrades that pass
            each gate individually, and all four gates (the PASS rate). A gate that
            does not apply counts as passed.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Vulnerability-fix rate</dt>
          <dd>
            The share of the {formatInteger(highlighted.vulnsFixable)} fixable
            vulnerability instances that are fixed; an instance whose rescan failed
            counts as not fixed.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Per-repository (macro) rates</dt>
          <dd>
            Repositories contribute 1 to 46 upgrades, so pooled rates, computed over
            all {formatInteger(highlighted.attempts)} upgrades together, lean towards
            a few large repositories. Macro rates average per-repository rates; they
            are reported for the PASS rate.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Paired comparisons</dt>
          <dd>
            For two agents on the same upgrades, the macro PASS-rate difference is
            tested for significance with a cluster bootstrap over repositories
            (10,000 resamples), which accounts for correlation within a
            repository.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Snyk-advice-solvable split</dt>
          <dd>
            The upgrades the package manager bump passes, a lower bound on what
            mechanical upgrading can solve. An agent's regressions are upgrades the
            bump passes and the agent fails; its gains, upgrades it passes and the
            bump fails. PASS rates are reported on both sides.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        All four harnesses ran on the same {formatInteger(highlighted.attempts)}
        {" "}upgrades from {highlighted.repos} repositories. The three agents share
        identical baselines ({formatInteger(highlighted.vulnsFixable)} fixable
        vulnerability instances in Snyk scans of 4–5 October 2026), with no
        infrastructure errors and no wall-clock timeouts. Four verification
        rescans failed (one for the package manager bump, one for Claude Code +
        Snyk scan, two for Claude Code unaided); those attempts count as failures.
        The package manager bump ran three days later, on {bumpRunDate}, and its
        {" "}{formatInteger(bump.attempts)} upgrades pair one-to-one with the
        agents': 305 have the same recommended upgrade, and two the same package
        with a newer target version.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site also reports cost and latency for the agents, which the paper
        does not. The Evo Remediation Agent's cost is estimated from its token
        counts at ${pricing.inputPerMillion} per
        million input and ${pricing.outputPerMillion} per million output tokens;
        Claude Code's is what the tool reports. The two methods are not directly
        comparable, and token counts are not comparable either. Latency is the
        agent's run alone.
      </p>

      <h2>Threats to validity</h2>
      <h3>Construct validity</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Snyk as task source and judge.</strong> Tasks and the rescan use
          Snyk's vulnerability database, which Claude Code unaided cannot consult,
          so a fixed version taken from another database may still be flagged.
          This penalises only Claude Code unaided; the Evo Remediation Agent and
          Claude Code + Snyk scan receive the same findings and target.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test strength.</strong> Breaking changes, especially changes in
          behaviour, are caught only as well as the project's tests exercise them.
          Six repositories (91 of the 307 upgrades) have at most four passing
          baseline tests, leaving most of the work to the build gate. VulnBump also
          relies only on each project's own tests, which agents can run, with no
          held-out tests or tests generated to target the upgrade. Held-out tests
          catch failures that visible ones miss: in DepBench, 61.8% of failed
          attempts had passed the visible tests or build. Even purpose-built tests
          catch only part: BreakGuard, which generates tests specifically to detect
          breaking dependency updates, catches at best 30.3% of those in the BUMP
          benchmark.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verifier noise.</strong> Test identities omit the module name, so
          same-named tests in different modules can mask regressions; three npm
          repositories without lockfiles let dependency trees float between
          baseline and verification; and the baseline is measured once, so a flaky
          test can be recorded as a regression (BUMP reruns tests three times).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A deliberately simple baseline.</strong> The package manager bump
          never moves a BOM or parent or tries another version, so the
          Snyk-advice-solvable split is a lower bound on what mechanical upgrading
          can solve, and the agents' gains overstate what only an agent can do.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>New vulnerabilities not penalised.</strong> They appear in more
          Evo Remediation Agent attempts than Claude Code attempts, so this favours
          the Evo Remediation Agent.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Internal validity</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Leakage.</strong> The GitHub and Snyk blocks are host-file based,
          without egress filtering, so DNS-over-HTTPS, hard-coded IPs, unlisted
          mirrors, web archives and search snippets can bypass them; more simply,
          about 25
          repositories are published libraries whose later releases are on package
          registries. Leakage would favour Claude Code, since the Evo Remediation
          Agent has no web tool and its prompt forbids such fetches. Claude Code
          transcripts were not kept, so leakage cannot be audited.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Training-data exposure.</strong> 40 of the 43 public repositories
          are pinned at commits that predate {model.name}'s training data cutoff
          (June 2026), so the model may have seen these projects, their later upgrades or fixes
          for the same vulnerabilities. This may inflate absolute pass rates, but
          all three agents share the model.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>External validity</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Case set selection.</strong> A few very large repositories, such
          as Apache Kafka and Spring Framework, were excluded, so VulnBump
          under-represents very large repositories and, since several are Gradle
          builds, Gradle.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scope.</strong> VulnBump covers the six package managers behind
          about 92% of production issue–project pairs (not Go, .NET, Ruby, PHP or
          pnpm), and scores upgrades one at a time rather than ordered and combined
          in one tree, as remediation is usually run in practice.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Conclusion validity</h3>
      <p>
        Each agent, and the package manager bump, ran once per upgrade, so the
        significance tests capture variation across repositories, not reruns.
        Upgrades are concentrated: the three largest repositories contribute 27%
        of them, and single repositories dominate some failure categories (one
        accounts for 28 of the 30 Claude Code dependency-gate failures), so pooled
        failure counts lean on a few projects, whereas macro rates weight
        repositories equally. Several package managers have only
        {" "}{Math.min(...repoCounts)} repositories, so per-package-manager estimates
        are imprecise. All counts are a snapshot of the vulnerability database at
        scan time.
      </p>
      <h3>Future work</h3>
      <p>
        The planned re-run of VulnBump adds repeated runs to measure run-to-run
        variance; three baseline test runs to detect flakiness; held-out tests
        that agents cannot run, to catch changes in behaviour; a whole-repository
        mode that applies all of a repository's upgrades together instead of each
        in isolation; a verdict that also penalises new vulnerabilities;
        egress-level leakage controls; further ecosystems such as Go, .NET and
        pnpm; more models, to separate the effect of the model from that of the
        agent; and retained transcripts, for failure-cause adjudication by several
        annotators with measured agreement.
      </p>

      <h2>Data availability</h2>
      <p>
        The VulnBump harness (task builder, sandbox, verifier and analysis
        scripts) and the 43 evaluation cases (repository, pinned commit, image and
        commands) are open source
        {benchmark.links.harness ? (
          <>
            {" "}at <a href={benchmark.links.harness}>{benchmark.links.harness.replace("https://", "")}</a>
          </>
        ) : null}. Tasks are derived from a Snyk scan at run time, which requires
        the Snyk CLI and a token. The repositories are pinned, but the
        vulnerability database is not, so a later run on the same commits will
        usually find more than the {formatInteger(highlighted.vulnsFixable)}
        {" "}fixable instances reported here (scanned 4–5 October 2026) and may
        recommend different targets. For example, CVE-2026-76183 (high severity),
        published between the last agent scan (5 October) and the package manager
        bump run (8 October), is fixed only from tomcat-embed-websocket 10.1.60, so
        two upgrades in tokuhirom/java-handbook target 10.1.60 for the package
        manager bump but 10.1.59 for the agents. Runs scanned on different days
        should therefore be compared only on tasks with the same recommended
        upgrade, and against their own baseline.
      </p>
      <p>
        The aggregated results are on the <a href="/data">data page</a>.
